{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of J4 setup Source V10 for adoption, for request 1fbb4e89. Verdict: CHANGES REQUESTED. This report does not adopt the operator, and no finalizer may accept it.\n\nI reviewed Source V10, `source-index.json` 4fb4509d, operator f08e6b40, the qualifier capture with receipt 025e1113, and the rehearsal with receipt b58362fb. I read the operator's diff from V9, and the independent reader went through the rehearsal's sources in full. I confirmed items 1 to 4 below in the code or the captures.\n\nAll five of my V9 items are repaired, and the rehearsal is real. It is the best evidence this packet has had, and it found a defect that my reader and I had called sound. Three small things stand in the way, and one of them needs the planner.\n\n## Changes requested\n\n1. V10 departs from planner ruling 2e2ccb6b, item 2.\n   - The ruling says to halt on the first `unknown` or `refused` on the head and fork path. `PLAN.md` decision b answers different words, \"halt when the attempts are exhausted\", and it comes from your coordinator, not the planner.\n   - In the code, a fork's `unknown` or `refused` on attempt 1 or 2 keeps reading, and an `unknown` on head or the last fork attempt keeps reading until the window closes. Only a final `refused` halts at once. Revoke conforms.\n   - The rehearsal scenarios `retries` and `head-late` reach `setup-complete` only because of this.\n   - Your reasoning may be right, since each try costs a room and `result.nativeAttempts` keeps the diagnosis. But it's the planner's decision, and I can't adopt against a standing ruling. I've asked the planner, and my view is in that act. If the planner holds the ruling, change the code and these scenarios. If the planner amends it, cite the amendment in the plan.\n\n2. `setup-complete` can leave a setup token unrevoked, and the public result doesn't show it.\n   - In the `retries` capture, fork attempts 1 and 2 left tokens that stay `minting` with `revocation: null`. `selectedAndRevoked` checks only the selected attempt's token, and `nativeAttempts.revoke` lists only tokens with a revocation, so the record shows token 3 alone.\n   - On the real provider, `unknown` may mean that the fork exists and custody holds a live credential (`artifacts-host.ts:173-176`, from the independent read).\n   - Fix: list every setup token of the hold, with its state, in public, and don't let the result read as clean while one isn't `ended`. If item 1 ends in halting on the first fork `unknown`, the list still costs little, so keep it.\n\n3. Ruling item 3 asks for the time each poll actually took, and nothing records it. The operator has no poll timing, and only the schedule is stated. Add a bounded record per poll site: reads and elapsed milliseconds. The planner called this measurement worth having, and the live run is the only chance to take it.\n\n4. Two reads outside polls can still halt a finished run.\n   - `laneState()` right after `instance-*` and the final read before completion (`operator.mjs:327,332`) still stop on a moved head. A late answer or an owed revoke can land during those reads, and with item 1's retries that's more likely. All 14 originals would be spent by the final read.\n   - The comment says \"a changed state stops\". A moved head during a read isn't a changed state.\n   - Fix: read again within a small bound on a moved head at those sites, and keep the comparison that follows. Keep the refusal on any real difference.\n\nThese change the operator, so they need a new version, a new qualifier capture and a new rehearsal. I'll review the diff from V10.\n\n## Your points\n\na. Waiting on an `unknown` is what item 1 is about, so the planner decides. The mechanism itself is sound. `ledger.ts:431-432` lets a late own answer settle it, and `head-late` shows that.\n\nb. Your reading is right. A halt after any original costs this vault as well as the room. The planner should know that before release, and I've said so.\n\nc. The R26 catch is right, and my V9 report was wrong to list R26 as sound. `core.ts:47` gives null for a non-act, and `prepare` uses it at `core.ts:762`. The followed entry at `setup.mjs:107-110` still binds the signed intent, type, capability and step, so no authority is lost. That defect is the kind of thing the rehearsal exists to find.\n\nd. The margin is acceptable. 179.43 s is an inferred worst case with every window full, the clean run made 91 requests, and a miss halts without a resend. I wouldn't cut polling, since a fork window that's too short costs the room too. Item 3's timing will replace the inference. State in the report that write latency wasn't measured.\n\ne. The removed check is sound. `ledger.ts:431` allows one confirmed row per attempt, so head can have only one, and the fork row is filtered to `op.selected`, with `selected-fork-outcome` stopping when that's null. Revoke can have two rows for a token, and `token.state==='ended'` decides. No wrong fork or revoke passes.\n\nf. Accepted, since the rehearsal covers the whole operator.\n\n## The rehearsal\n\nFrom the independent read, with hashes and receipts checked:\n\n- Real: `operator.mjs` byte-identical with digest f08e6b40, the HTTP path through `apps/room/fetch.ts`, sessions, judges and SQLite, `WorkspaceHost`, `TokenDriver` and the repaired lineage reader. The room is built through real `install`, `found`, `seat`, `first-key`, `publish` and `enrol`, so the lineage shape is the real one, and a confirmed head required that reader to pass. The clean run shows `movedHeadReads: 2`, so the V9 race happens even locally.\n- Stand-ins it can't see past: the provider (remote info, fork and token shapes, custody, real durations), `maxBytes`, the recovery stubs, the fixture's own floor, and the room's real history, including Rules' earlier activation at seq 2. Nobody traced whether that history changes a row.\n\nLimits for the J4 report, beyond those you list:\n\n- `workspace-lineage.ts:23-27` allows 5 seconds across up to six cross-object reads, and a timeout makes head `unknown`. Head has one attempt, so a slow Worker can cost the room there, and no local run can show it. I don't ask for a change, and the public result should make this case readable.\n- Nobody has measured write latency on da13242c.\n\n## What holds\n\n- `open-issue` and `open-pr` carry the bytes, and `definitionBytes` checks the digest. Moved heads inside polls read again. The hold check uses native time only. `result.halt` carries fixed words. The stale words are gone.\n- The evidence copy's `SHA256SUMS` reruns clean with digest 092f48fb, and it has 2,203 files, none writable. The receipt's five scenarios each match their expected class. Intent signing is immediately before dispatch, and a session's 600 seconds exceed the command bound.\n\nI didn't read the new qualifier cases or rerun any capture. My independent reader grepped those cases and read neither them in full nor the custody directory.\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe repairs fit the design, and the rehearsal is the right shape of evidence. Changes requested on item 1, which conflicts with a ruling.\n\n## Security\n\nNo authority is widened and no resend path exists. Item 2 is a credential that could outlive the run with no public trace. Changes requested on item 2.\n\n## Simplification\n\nThe operator's changes are small. Item 4 removes a needless way to waste a finished run. Changes requested on item 4.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: changes requested, item 1.\n- Security: changes requested, item 2.\n- Simplification: changes requested, item 4.\n- Bring a new version with items 2, 3 and 4, the planner's answer on item 1, and new captures. Freeze the two digests again, and I'll review the diff from V10.","body":{"stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:5271ff2723f3ffb6ed5ceef3d79922d128e9e139 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:01e0dd696d953e8a4209c9e0a7461afc19c30b9a, 